#!/bin/sh
# why-shell: a git hook body, and the one script here that cannot move to the TS lane or into the
# kit — git runs hooks from the repo whose commits they gate, so every consumer must carry this
# file itself.
#
# So it carries as little as possible. The gate is the kit's `tools/pre-commit-gate.sh`
# (`vp staged --no-stash`, a per-worktree backup ref, the cross-worktree commit lock — `◊WTH-03`);
# this is the seam that reaches it. It is the LAST place a consumer spells the kit's path itself:
# the session hooks carried the same shape in `.claude/settings.json` until `◊IL-07` moved them to a
# plugin that resolves the kit from its own location, needing no variable at all. A git hook cannot
# be delivered that way. A consumer adopts the gate by copying these lines once, and picks up every
# later change to it by doing nothing (`◊WTH-02`). Sourced rather than spawned, so it needs nothing
# on PATH that the gate does not need anyway.
#
# Every id here is BACKTICK-QUOTED, which is the id gate's escape, and both spellings need it: these
# bytes live in five repos at once. A bare sigil id resolves against whichever tracker reads the
# line — the kit's here, a consumer's after the copy, where these ids do not exist — and a consumer's
# gate reads a changed line as newly introduced, so an unquoted sigil reddens every re-copy. The
# qualified `jnana-kit:<ID>` form inverts the problem: correct in a consumer, and rejected in the kit
# as self-qualification. No unquoted spelling is right in both, and quoting says so honestly — no
# gate can resolve a citation whose home repo depends on who is reading.
#
# Fails OPEN to a bare `vp staged` when the kit is not resolvable: a checkout without the kit is a
# machine without pool slots, where lint-staged's own backup is the right one and only the
# serialization is missing. Loudly, though — a gate that quietly changed shape is how `◊FND-92` went
# unnoticed for a whole effort.
#
# `$JNANA_KIT_HOME` is the whole of how the kit is found here, with no default behind it: this line
# used to guess `$HOME/Sites/jnana-kit`, which was right for one machine layout and silently wrong
# for every other. Unset now takes the same loud open arm below as a wrong path, which already
# names the variable as the fix.
KIT="${JNANA_KIT_HOME:-}"
if [ -r "$KIT/tools/pre-commit-gate.sh" ]; then
    . "$KIT/tools/pre-commit-gate.sh"
else
    printf 'pre-commit: no jnana-kit gate at %s — running vp staged bare: no cross-worktree commit lock, and lint-staged backs its work up onto the repo-shared refs/stash. Set JNANA_KIT_HOME.\n' "$KIT" >&2
    exec vp staged
fi
